 2. 🚁 Delivery drones get shushed 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Maura Losch/Axios Drone delivery companies are scrambling to turn down the volume as neighborhood opposition threatens to ground their expansion plans, Axios' Joann Muller reports. 

 📦 Amazon last week abandoned plans for a drone delivery hub in suburban Boston after residents and lawmakers raised concerns about noise, safety and privacy.

 The company withdrew its application in Westborough, Mass., but left open the possibility of returning. Rival Zipline says it has taught its drones to fly more quietly.

 Zipline's "dynamic flying" software lets each drone pick the quietest route in real time based on wind, speed and altitude, allowing drones to glide more efficiently, like a bird, instead of relying on noisy propellers. ✅ Reality check: Quieter doesn't necessarily mean quiet. 

 As drone deliveries multiply, even relatively unobtrusive aircraft could become an unwelcome neighborhood nuisance. Go deeper ... Get Axios Future of Mobility . 

 
 
