 4. 🌾 1 for the road: Carter family farm protected 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Photo: Addison Hill/Courtesy of The Conservation Fund The southwest Georgia landscape that helped shape President Jimmy Carter's commitment to conservation is set to become permanently protected as public land, Axios Atlanta's Thomas Wheatley reports. 

 The Conservation Fund's purchase of 2,458 acres of farmland and forests — including the Carter family's property outside Plains, Ga. — carries on an oft-overshadowed part of the humanitarian's legacy. President Jimmy Carter carries a peanut plant in 1978 as he and the first lady, Rosalynn, walk in a field at their farm near Plains, Ga. Photo: Jim Wells/AP 🌳 Stacy Funderburke , the nonprofit's vice president of the central Southeast region, said in a statement: "Long before Jimmy Carter protected millions of acres across America, he was a boy working the fields and roaming the woods of southwest Georgia."

 "This land shaped him and his legacy, and he never stopped coming home to it." Go deeper . 

 
 
